!deck: Math::Real-Numbers
!tags: Math Real-Numbers Axiom Ordered-Field
!type: Cloze Math

!id: I90z8Gopoo
!text:
The {{c2::associative law for addition::axiom name}} field axiom for \( \mathbb{R} \) states that

{{c1::for every \( a,b,c\in\mathbb{R} \), \( a+(b+c)=(a+b)+c \)::axiom statement}}.
~~~

!id: OLnjdCyRHg
!text:
The {{c2::existence of additive identity::axiom name}} field axiom for \( \mathbb{R} \) states that

{{c1::there exists \( 0\in\mathbb{R} \) such that \( a+0=0+a=a \) for every \( a\in\mathbb{R} \)::axiom statement}}.
~~~

!id: JgllfeEwx0
!text:
The {{c2::existence of additive inverse::axiom name}} field axiom for \( \mathbb{R} \) states that

{{c1::for every \( a\in\mathbb{R} \), there exists an additive inverse \( -a\in\mathbb{R} \) such that \( a+(-a)=(-a)+a=0 \)::axiom statement}}.
~~~

!id: N2NZXpYhOd
!text:
The {{c2::commutative law for addition::axiom name}} field axiom for \( \mathbb{R} \) states that

{{c1::for every \( a,b\in\mathbb{R} \), \( a+b=b+a \)::axiom statement}}.
~~~

!id: hgVaALsDZj
!text:
The {{c2::associative law for multiplication::axiom name}} field axiom for \( \mathbb{R} \) states that

{{c1::for every \( a,b,c\in\mathbb{R} \), \( a(bc)=(ab)c \)::axiom statement}}.
~~~

!id: bNcoGRl039
!text:
The {{c2::existence of multiplicative identity::axiom name}} field axiom for \( \mathbb{R} \) states that

{{c1::there exists \( 1\in\mathbb{R} \) with \( 1\ne0 \) such that \( a\cdot1=1\cdot a=a \) for every \( a\in\mathbb{R} \)::axiom statement}}.
~~~

!id: ih99MacoIR
!text:
The {{c2::existence of multiplicative inverse::axiom name}} field axiom for \( \mathbb{R} \) states that

{{c1::for every \( a\in\mathbb{R} \) with \( a\ne0 \), there exists an inverse \( a^{-1}\in\mathbb{R} \) satisfying \( aa^{-1}=a^{-1}a=1 \)::axiom statement}}.
~~~

!id: U5HyBbwUOW
!text:
The {{c2::commutative law for multiplication::axiom name}} field axiom for \( \mathbb{R} \) states that

{{c1::for every \( a,b\in\mathbb{R} \), \( ab=ba \)::axiom statement}}.
~~~

!id: GsI5Rl4JC0
!text:
The {{c2::distributive law::axiom name}} field axiom for \( \mathbb{R} \) states that

{{c1::for every \( a,b,c\in\mathbb{R} \), \( a(b+c)=ab+ac \)::axiom statement}}.
~~~

!id: J0HOkKSIRf
!text:
The {{c2::trichotomy::axiom name}} order axiom for \( \mathbb{R} \) states that

{{c1::for every \( a,b\in\mathbb{R} \), exactly one of \( a<b \), \( a=b \), and \( b<a \) holds::axiom statement}}.
~~~

!id: cKOb9STugi
!text:
The {{c2::transitivity::axiom name}} order axiom for \( \mathbb{R} \) states that

{{c1::for every \( a,b,c\in\mathbb{R} \), if \( a<b \) and \( b<c \), then \( a<c \)::axiom statement}}.
~~~

!id: WZEHxT7bcf
!text:
The {{c2::compatibility with addition::axiom name}} order axiom for \( \mathbb{R} \) states that

{{c1::for every \( a,b,c\in\mathbb{R} \), if \( a<b \), then \( a+c<b+c \)::axiom statement}}.
~~~

!id: nHxVuoJ0fm
!text:
The {{c2::compatibility with multiplication by a positive number::axiom name}} order axiom for \( \mathbb{R} \) states that

{{c1::for every \( a,b,c\in\mathbb{R} \), if \( a<b \) and \( 0<c \), then \( ac<bc \)::axiom statement}}.
~~~

!type: Basic Math

!id: Z9s9uDJry1
!front:
Which statement expresses the additive identity axiom for \( \mathbb{R} \)?

1. For every \( a\in\mathbb{R} \), there exists \( e\in\mathbb{R} \) such that \( a+e=e+a=a \).
2. There exists \( e\in\mathbb{R} \) such that, for every \( a\in\mathbb{R} \), \( a+e=e+a=a \).
!back:
\[ \boxed{\text{Statement 2}} \]

One identity \( e \), denoted \( 0 \), must work for every \( a \).

Statement 1 is also true in \( \mathbb{R} \), but allows \( e \) to depend on \( a \), so it does not require a shared identity.
~~~

!id: GGRTHUxfa4
!front:
Which statement expresses the multiplicative identity axiom for \( \mathbb{R} \)?

1. There exists \( e\in\mathbb{R} \) with \( e\ne0 \) such that \( ae=ea=a \) for every \( a\in\mathbb{R} \).
2. For every \( a\in\mathbb{R} \), there exists \( e\in\mathbb{R} \) with \( e\ne0 \) such that \( ae=ea=a \).
!back:
\[ \boxed{\text{Statement 1}} \]

One nonzero identity \( e \), denoted \( 1 \), must work for every \( a \).

Statement 2 is also true in \( \mathbb{R} \), but allows \( e \) to depend on \( a \), so it does not require a shared identity.
~~~

!id: Z4BwfVRLXs
!front:
Which statement expresses the additive inverse axiom for \( \mathbb{R} \), with additive identity \( 0 \)?

1. There exists \( b\in\mathbb{R} \) such that, for every \( a\in\mathbb{R} \), \( a+b=b+a=0 \).
2. For every \( a\in\mathbb{R} \), there exists \( b\in\mathbb{R} \) such that \( a+b=b+a=0 \).
!back:
\[ \boxed{\text{Statement 2}} \]

The inverse \( b=-a \) may depend on \( a \).

Statement 1 requires one \( b \) to invert every real number; \( a=0 \) requires \( b=0 \), while \( a=1 \) requires \( b=-1 \).
~~~

!id: okSvWlvO6W
!front:
Which statement expresses the multiplicative inverse axiom for \( \mathbb{R} \)?

1. For every nonzero \( a\in\mathbb{R} \), there exists \( b\in\mathbb{R} \) such that \( ab=ba=1 \).
2. There exists \( b\in\mathbb{R} \) such that, for every nonzero \( a\in\mathbb{R} \), \( ab=ba=1 \).
!back:
\[ \boxed{\text{Statement 1}} \]

The inverse \( b=a^{-1} \) may depend on \( a \).

Statement 2 requires one \( b \) to invert every nonzero real number; \( a=1 \) requires \( b=1 \), while \( a=2 \) requires \( b=1/2 \).
~~~
